<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> skiing and snowboarding come with their own vocabulary. A bluebird day is sunny after a storm, corduroy is freshly groomed snow, a powder day means fresh, untracked snow, and après is the social time after skiing. Here are 70+ common terms, alphabetized, in plain English.</p></div>

<h2>A–C</h2>
<p><strong>Après-ski:</strong> the social time after the lifts close: food, drinks, music, or a hot tub. More in <%= link_to "what is après-ski", guide_path("what-is-apres-ski") %>.</p>
<p><strong>Avalanche beacon (transceiver):</strong> a device worn in avalanche terrain that sends and receives a signal so partners can find a buried person.</p>
<p><strong>Backcountry:</strong> terrain outside resort boundaries that isn't patrolled or avalanche-controlled.</p>
<p><strong>Base:</strong> the bottom of the mountain, or the depth of settled snow on the ground. Also the bottom surface of a ski or snowboard.</p>
<p><strong>Binding:</strong> the device that attaches your boot to a ski or snowboard.</p>
<p><strong>Black diamond:</strong> an advanced run in North America. See <%= link_to "ski trail ratings", guide_path("ski-trail-ratings") %>.</p>
<p><strong>Bluebird day:</strong> a clear, sunny day, especially right after a snowstorm.</p>
<p><strong>Bunny hill:</strong> a gentle beginner slope, often served by a magic carpet or slow lift.</p>
<p><strong>Camber:</strong> a ski or board profile that arches up in the middle, giving grip and pop.</p>
<p><strong>Carving:</strong> turning on the edges so the ski or board cuts a clean arc rather than skidding.</p>
<p><strong>Catwalk:</strong> a narrow, flat trail that connects runs, often cut across the mountain.</p>
<p><strong>Chairlift:</strong> an aerial lift with open chairs that carries skiers uphill.</p>
<p><strong>Chatter:</strong> vibration in skis or a board at speed, often on hard snow.</p>
<p><strong>Chute:</strong> a narrow, steep passage, usually between rocks.</p>
<p><strong>Cornice:</strong> an overhanging lip of wind-blown snow on a ridge. Cornices can collapse.</p>
<p><strong>Corduroy:</strong> freshly groomed snow with ridged lines that look like corduroy fabric.</p>
<p><strong>Crud:</strong> choppy, uneven snow that's been skied up or affected by wind or weather.</p>

<h2>D–G</h2>
<p><strong>DIN setting:</strong> the number on a ski binding that controls how much force it takes to release your boot. Set by a shop based on height, weight, age, and ability.</p>
<p><strong>Double black diamond:</strong> expert terrain on North American trail maps.</p>
<p><strong>Dump:</strong> a big snowfall.</p>
<p><strong>Edge:</strong> the metal strip along the sides of skis and boards that grips the snow.</p>
<p><strong>Face shot:</strong> powder deep enough to spray into your face as you turn.</p>
<p><strong>Fall line:</strong> the most direct path straight down a slope.</p>
<p><strong>First chair:</strong> the first lift ride of the day, prized on powder days.</p>
<p><strong>First tracks:</strong> the first turns down untracked snow.</p>
<p><strong>Flat light:</strong> cloudy or foggy light that makes it hard to see bumps and changes in the snow.</p>
<p><strong>Freeride:</strong> skiing or riding natural, varied terrain off groomed runs.</p>
<p><strong>Freestyle:</strong> tricks, jumps, rails, and park riding.</p>
<p><strong>Gaper:</strong> slang for an obvious newcomer, often with a gap between goggles and helmet ("gaper gap").</p>
<p><strong>Glade:</strong> a run through trees.</p>
<p><strong>Gondola:</strong> an enclosed lift cabin that carries passengers uphill.</p>
<p><strong>Goofy:</strong> riding a snowboard with your right foot forward.</p>
<p><strong>Groomer:</strong> a machine that smooths snow, or a groomed run itself.</p>

<h2>H–M</h2>
<p><strong>Hardpack:</strong> firm, compacted snow.</p>
<p><strong>Heelside:</strong> the snowboard edge under your heels.</p>
<p><strong>Hockey stop:</strong> a fast stop made by turning both skis sideways to the fall line.</p>
<p><strong>Ice coast:</strong> nickname for the East Coast, where firm, icy conditions are common.</p>
<p><strong>Inbounds:</strong> terrain within a resort's boundaries that's patrolled.</p>
<p><strong>Kicker:</strong> a jump, usually in a terrain park.</p>
<p><strong>Lift line:</strong> the queue for a lift, or the run beneath a lift.</p>
<p><strong>Liftie:</strong> a lift operator.</p>
<p><strong>Magic carpet:</strong> a conveyor-belt lift on beginner slopes.</p>
<p><strong>Moguls (bumps):</strong> mounds of snow formed by skiers turning in the same spots. See <%= link_to "how to ski moguls", guide_path("how-to-ski-moguls") %>.</p>

<h2>N–P</h2>
<p><strong>Nordic skiing:</strong> cross-country skiing, where the heel is free.</p>
<p><strong>Off-piste:</strong> ungroomed terrain away from marked runs; in Europe it is often outside patrolled areas.</p>
<p><strong>On-piste:</strong> on marked, groomed runs.</p>
<p><strong>Packed powder:</strong> powder that's been compressed by skiers or grooming.</p>
<p><strong>Park rat:</strong> someone who spends most of their time in the terrain park.</p>
<p><strong>Piste:</strong> a marked, groomed ski run (common in Europe).</p>
<p><strong>Pizza:</strong> the wedge position for slowing down, ski tips together and tails apart.</p>
<p><strong>Pole plant:</strong> touching your pole to the snow to time a turn.</p>
<p><strong>Pow / powder:</strong> fresh, soft, untracked snow.</p>
<p><strong>Powder day:</strong> a day with fresh snow, usually after a big overnight storm. See <%= link_to "how to chase powder", guide_path("how-to-chase-powder") %>.</p>
<p><strong>Pow-pow:</strong> slang for very good powder.</p>

<h2>R–S</h2>
<p><strong>Regular:</strong> riding a snowboard with your left foot forward.</p>
<p><strong>Rocker:</strong> a profile with tips and tails that rise, making skis and boards float and turn easily.</p>
<p><strong>Rope tow:</strong> a moving rope you hold to be pulled uphill.</p>
<p><strong>Schuss:</strong> to ski straight down without turning.</p>
<p><strong>Send it:</strong> slang for committing fully to a jump or line.</p>
<p><strong>Shred:</strong> to ski or ride aggressively and well.</p>
<p><strong>Sidecountry:</strong> backcountry terrain reached from a resort lift, outside the boundary and not patrolled.</p>
<p><strong>Ski patrol:</strong> the team that handles safety, first aid, and avalanche control.</p>
<p><strong>Skins:</strong> strips attached to ski bases that grip snow for climbing uphill.</p>
<p><strong>Slush:</strong> wet, heavy snow, common on warm spring days.</p>
<p><strong>Snowcat:</strong> a tracked vehicle used for grooming or carrying skiers to terrain.</p>
<p><strong>Snowmaking:</strong> machine-made snow produced by blowing water into cold air.</p>
<p><strong>Splitboard:</strong> a snowboard that splits into two skis for climbing.</p>
<p><strong>Sticks:</strong> slang for skis.</p>
<p><strong>Switch:</strong> riding or skiing backward from your normal direction.</p>

<h2>T–Z</h2>
<p><strong>Tele (telemark):</strong> a style of skiing with a free heel and a lunging turn.</p>
<p><strong>Terrain park:</strong> an area with jumps, rails, and boxes for freestyle.</p>
<p><strong>Toeside:</strong> the snowboard edge under your toes.</p>
<p><strong>Touring:</strong> climbing uphill on skis or a splitboard to ski down.</p>
<p><strong>Traverse:</strong> skiing across the slope rather than down it.</p>
<p><strong>Tree well:</strong> a deep, loose pocket of snow around a tree trunk that can trap a fallen skier. Ski trees with a partner.</p>
<p><strong>Tuning:</strong> sharpening edges and waxing the base of skis or a board.</p>
<p><strong>Twin tip:</strong> a ski or board with upturned tips at both ends, for riding switch.</p>
<p><strong>Vertical (vert):</strong> the elevation difference between a resort's base and summit.</p>
<p><strong>Wax:</strong> a coating applied to the base so skis and boards glide.</p>
<p><strong>Whiteout:</strong> snow or fog so thick you can't see the terrain.</p>
<p><strong>Yard sale:</strong> a crash that scatters your skis, poles, and gear across the slope.</p>

<p>Ready to put the vocabulary to use? Start with <%= link_to "how to ski", guide_path("how-to-ski") %> or <%= link_to "how to snowboard", guide_path("how-to-snowboard") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "What is a bluebird day in skiing?", answer: "A bluebird day is a clear, sunny day, especially the first sunny day after a snowstorm, when fresh snow and good visibility come together." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What does corduroy mean in skiing?", answer: "Corduroy is freshly groomed snow. The grooming machine leaves fine ridges that look like corduroy fabric." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What is a yard sale on the slopes?", answer: "A yard sale is a crash where your skis, poles, and other gear end up scattered across the slope." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "What is the difference between backcountry and sidecountry?", answer: "Both are outside resort boundaries and not patrolled. Sidecountry is reached from a resort lift; backcountry usually means terrain reached by hiking or touring from outside the resort." } %>
